# Durable resolver for the media namespace, as an extra systemd-resolved stub
# listener. (PRODUCTION-DNS-RESOLVER-001)
#
# Install to /etc/systemd/resolved.conf.d/10-videofetch-media-dns.conf and
# `systemctl restart systemd-resolved`.
#
# ── Why systemd-resolved rather than a new daemon ─────────────────────────
#
# It is already installed, already running, already the VM's resolver, and it
# already follows the host's upstream configuration — so the namespace inherits
# whatever the VM legitimately uses instead of a public resolver baked in here.
# A second resolver process would duplicate all of that and add a component to
# keep patched.
#
# ── The address is NOT arbitrary ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# It must be character-for-character the address declared as
# `--dns <address>` in VIDEOFETCH_MEDIA_DNS_FLAGS (media-egress.env), because
# that same string is what the namespace holder is created with, what the
# namespace resolv.conf names, and what the rendered nftables policy admits on
# UDP and TCP 53. Changing it here alone produces a resolver nothing is allowed
# to talk to; changing it in media-egress.env changes the rendered ruleset and
# therefore the policy fingerprint, which is a safe-egress topology change
# requiring re-acceptance, not a configuration tweak.
#
# vf-media-dns-check probes the address from media-egress.env, so a mismatch
# between these two files fails the readiness unit rather than passing quietly.
#
# ── Ordering is a non-issue, and that was verified, not assumed ───────────
#
# systemd-resolved starts several seconds BEFORE docker.service, so docker0 —
# and therefore this address — does not exist yet when the stub listener binds.
# It binds anyway: systemd sets IP_FREEBIND on stub listener sockets. This was
# confirmed empirically on the target VM, and the listener was observed to
# survive a systemd-resolved restart, a docker restart that destroys and
# recreates docker0, docker0 being taken administratively down and back up, and
# a full VM reboot.
#
# ── Exposure ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# DNSStubListenerExtra binds ONLY the address given. It does not listen on
# 0.0.0.0, on the VM's LAN address, or on any other interface, so this does not
# expose a resolver to the network the VM sits on. Verify after installing:
#
#   ss -lnup | grep ':53'      # expect the exact address, never 0.0.0.0:53
#   ss -lnt  | grep ':53'
#
# ── Ships UNFILLED, like media-egress.env.example ─────────────────────────
#
# No deployment address is committed to this repository, for the same reason
# the nftables template carries a @@VIDEOFETCH_DESIGNATED_DNS@@ marker rather
# than a resolver: a committed address is one that can silently disagree with
# the host it is installed on. Fill in the value below with the SAME address
# declared in VIDEOFETCH_MEDIA_DNS_FLAGS, then restart systemd-resolved.
#
# Agreement between the two files is not asserted textually — it is proved at
# runtime. vf-media-dns-check reads the address from media-egress.env and
# probes THAT address, so a drop-in that binds anything else fails the
# readiness unit and, through it, refuses to start the Worker.

[Resolve]
DNSStubListenerExtra=
